Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant tables / columns and how we will use them  
• `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations`  
  – `id`: station identifier – what we finally need to return  
  – `name`: human‑readable station name – what we finally need to return  
  – `latitude`, `longitude`: geographic coordinates – needed to calculate distance to the centre of Chicago  
  – `state`: two‑letter state code – may be useful to restrict to Illinois (“IL”) first  
• `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_states`  
  – `code`, `name`: lets us turn the state code into its long name (optional)  
• `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_inventory`  
  – `id`, `element`, `firstyear`, `lastyear`: lets us check what kind of elements (TMAX, TMIN, etc.) a station reports and since when (optional)

Typical filter / join conditions we will use  
1. Convert each latitude/longitude pair to a GEOGRAPHY with `ST_GeogPoint(longitude, latitude)`.  
2. Compute straight‑line distance with the Haversine formula that `ST_Distance` already implements:  

   ```
   ST_Distance(
       ST_GeogPoint(-87.6847, 41.8319),      -- Chicago centre
       ST_GeogPoint(longitude, latitude) )   -- station
   ```
3. Keep only stations where the distance ≤ 50 000 (metres) → 50 km.  
4. Optional filters: `state = 'IL'`, `element = 'TMAX'`, etc.

Below are 10 exploratory → increasingly complex SELECT queries (each capped at 20 rows) that one could run in BigQuery to understand the data and finally list the stations within a 50‑km radius of Chicago.  
(All identifiers are wrapped in back‑ticks; only tables listed under the allowed dataset are referenced.)

```sql
-- 1) Preview the stations table
SELECT
  id,
  name,
  latitude,
  longitude,
  state
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Which states occur in the stations table?
--    (Helps confirm the IL state code exists, etc.)
SELECT DISTINCT state
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations`
WHERE state IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Sample of Illinois (“IL”) stations with their coordinates
SELECT
  id,
  name,
  latitude,
  longitude
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations`
WHERE state = 'IL'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Add a computed distance (in metres) from Chicago centre
SELECT
  id,
  name,
  ST_Distance(
      ST_GeogPoint(-87.6847, 41.8319),          -- Chicago
      ST_GeogPoint(longitude, latitude) ) AS dist_m
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations`
WHERE state = 'IL'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Show the 20 nearest Illinois stations to downtown Chicago
SELECT
  id,
  name,
  dist_m
FROM (
  SELECT
    id,
    name,
    ST_Distance(
        ST_GeogPoint(-87.6847, 41.8319),
        ST_GeogPoint(longitude, latitude) ) AS dist_m
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations`
  WHERE state = 'IL'
)
ORDER BY dist_m
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Limit to those within 50 km and show distance in km (rounded)
SELECT
  id,
  name,
  ROUND(dist_m/1000,2) AS distance_km
FROM (
  SELECT
    id,
    name,
    ST_Distance(
        ST_GeogPoint(-87.6847, 41.8319),
        ST_GeogPoint(longitude, latitude) ) AS dist_m
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations`
)
WHERE dist_m <= 50000
ORDER BY distance_km
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) How many distinct stations are inside the 50‑km radius?
SELECT
  COUNT(DISTINCT id) AS stations_within_50km
FROM (
  SELECT id
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations`
  WHERE ST_Distance(
          ST_GeogPoint(-87.6847, 41.8319),
          ST_GeogPoint(longitude, latitude) ) <= 50000
);
```

```sql
-- 8) Join in the full state name from ghcnd_states
SELECT
  s.id,
  s.name AS station_name,
  st.name AS state_name,
  ROUND(
    ST_Distance(
        ST_GeogPoint(-87.6847, 41.8319),
        ST_GeogPoint(s.longitude, s.latitude) )/1000,2) AS distance_km
FROM `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations` AS s
LEFT JOIN `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_states` AS st
       ON s.state = st.code
WHERE ST_Distance(
        ST_GeogPoint(-87.6847, 41.8319),
        ST_GeogPoint(s.longitude, s.latitude) ) <= 50000
ORDER BY distance_km
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Attach earliest year the station ever recorded TMAX, as an example
SELECT
  near.id,
  near.name AS station_name,
  MIN(inv.firstyear) AS first_TMAX_year
FROM (
  SELECT id, name
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_stations`
  WHERE ST_Distance(
          ST_GeogPoint(-87.6847, 41.8319),
          ST_GeogPoint(longitude, latitude) ) <= 50000
) AS near
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.ghcn_d.ghcnd_inventory` AS inv
  ON near.id = inv.id
WHERE inv.element = 'TMAX'
GROUP BY near.id, near.name
LIMIT 20;
```

